PART I: THE SWEPT HEARTH AND THE STRATEGY

The firepit in the center of the monastery floor held no wood and no ash, the flagstones swept clean to bare stone. Four old men in grey robes sat on the floor at the four quarters of the room, their eyes shut and their hands resting on their knees. They did not look up when packs were cinched. They did not move when boots crossed the stone.

Esbern pinned his satchel shut, his knuckles white against the leather. He looked at the four monks, then across the floor at Orion and Davinia.

"Thirty years I hid in the dark," he said, his voice hard against the vault of the ceiling. "I told myself if we reached this mountain, there would be an order waiting. Masters who would stand with us. And they sit on the floor while Alduin raises dragons from their graves across Skyrim. Mortal iron cannot kill them permanently. When Alduin calls their names, the bones knit back whole. You need a Dragonborn to draw the soul out of the world, and there is no Dragonborn." He took two paces toward the doorway. "What is our strategy when we walk back out that door? How do we fight a dragon war with ordinary mortal steel?"

Orion adjusted the strap on his quiver. "One step at a time," he said. "We're an improv team. We figure it out as we go."

Bjorn stood by the empty hearth, staring down at the stone. He did not look at Esbern when he spoke.

"Stop talking, all of you," Bjorn said. His voice was flat and dry. "Mable knew the road down that mountain better than any man in this room. Every turn. Her guts are frozen to the floor of a troll den twenty minutes down the path, and four holy men watched us walk in here half-frozen and did not move to light a fire." He looked up at Saijah, then Davinia. "We couldn't protect an old horse. How are we supposed to protect anyone else?"

The hall went quiet. Saijah stepped forward, her voice low. "We try our best to avenge her, Bjorn. That is what we have left."

Bjorn shook his head. "Avenge her on an animal? What does that do? She can't go anywhere anymore. Why should I?"

"Because she would have gone anywhere with you," Saijah said. "And there are places we haven't reached yet."

Saijah looked between them, then turned to the doorway. "Arngeir gave us the words. When we reach that den, the threshold opens only when every voice speaks as one in True Need: Tah Joor Nir, Thu'um Ven Su'um. The dragon tongue."

Orion watched Bjorn standing alone by the hearth. He crossed the distance and gripped the older man's shoulder. "You're coming with us, Bjorn. At the very least, you stay for Mila."

Bjorn stood still for a long breath, his jaw set. Then he reached down, took Mila's hand, and walked toward the heavy timber doors.

PART II: THE FLAME ON THE HAND

Alfonso walked apart from the rest, his presence anchored in two hundred and six obsidian bones that clicked with the dry rattle of stones on gravel. The Jagged Crown hung from his hand, dark iron and ancient teeth.

He looked at the crown, then at the company gathering their packs. "I have spent months spreading sickness," he said, his skull turning toward the empty hall. "I think I will pivot. People need belief in something. If everyone dies anyway, let there be someone who meets them at the end."

He closed his eyes and pushed his consciousness through the back door into Davinia's mind.

The memory opened into an Imperial ballroom. Davinia stood in a heavy, stiff ball gown of layered silk, seventeen years old, her hands trembling as she adjusted the bodice. Shadows crowded the corners of the hall where an Imperial general stood watching her. 

Beside her in the glass of the tall mirror stood Ismara, wearing a matching gown of pale lace. Ismara stepped between Davinia and the memory of the general, speaking soft reassurances, rewriting the fear into safety, sliding herself into the gap where Davinia's mother had once stood.

Alfonso stepped into the reflection behind them.

Davinia turned in the memory, looking at the black skeleton standing among the silk. "Alfonso?"

"I'm around," Alfonso said. He looked at the general, then down at Davinia's hand. "I am leaving you something."

He raised his hand. Dunmer Ancestor's Fire ignited along his phalanges, white-hot and silent. He flicked his finger, sending a single ember across the distance.

The fire landed on the back of Davinia's hand. It sank through the skin without pain, leaving a distinct, glowing burn mark set into the flesh.

"Whenever you look down," Alfonso said, "you will see that mark. You will know you are not alone, and you will know when a memory is being rewritten."

Ismara stared at the glowing mark in the glass, her expression hardening as the working settled beyond her reach.

Alfonso pulled his consciousness back to the monastery hall, the ember cooling in his palm.

PART III: THE DESCENT AND THE HUNTER'S SCENT

The monastery doors pushed open into the gale. The summit wind struck them full in the chest, driving snow across the grey slabs.

Saijah took the lead down the ridge, bow in hand, her eyes scanning the whiteout below. Ylva fell into step beside her, her heavy battleaxe resting across her shoulders.

They walked in silence for fifty paces before Ylva spoke, her voice cutting through the roar of the wind.

"How close did he get?" Ylva asked.

Saijah kept her eyes on the iced steps. "What?"

"The stag," Ylva said, turning her head slightly. "When you followed it off the road. How close did it let you walk?"

"I was right beside it," Saijah said.

Ylva's jaw tightened. "Fifteen years. I tracked that scent through the Reach and the Rift every winter of my life. He never let me within three hundred yards before the wind turned. And you walked beside him like you were sharing a cart."

Saijah stopped on the step. "I never asked for him to follow me, Ylva. I don't even know if I want what he offers."

Ylva stepped into her path, towering over her. "My god is in your skull, Saijah. I took his blood, and it gave me power you cannot imagine. When that door opens below, it opens once. He will offer you the blood. If you pretend you don't want it, you will die on that mountain. Decide before we reach the cave."

Ylva turned and stalked down the path toward the rising steam.

PART IV: THE WIND TUNNEL AND THE TROLL'S DEN

The path narrowed into a high mountain shelf between a sheer rock wall on the left and a concave overhang of ice on the right. The wind funneled through the corridor like a flume, dropping visibility to twenty feet in driving white.

Under the sheltered overhang, the wind died away. Dark steam rose from the floor of a shallow cave. 

Something stood up in the dark. It rose until its head scraped the stone ceiling nine feet above the ground--matted white fur crusted with black sap, three milky eyes, and forearms as thick as timber logs. Between its feet lay Mable's frozen blood-trail.

Nora dropped to one knee behind a rock, fire crackling in her palms. A deep reservoir of gathered flame burned at her fingertips.

"Don't release all of it at once," Orion warned, catching her arm. "That explosion will take you with it."

Nora nodded, her teeth gritted. She leveled her hand and cast Firebolt into the cave mouth.

The stored fire tore through the drift as one column of flame and struck the troll full in the chest with a concussive crack, burning deep into its thick, sap-crusted hide. The creature roared, the impact driving it back against the stone wall as flames caught its fur.

Orion raised his horn and blew a resonant, piercing blast that echoed through the tunnel, staggering the beast and stripping its focus. Ylva surged forward through the snow, her battleaxe cleaving into the beast's flank to sunder its hide.

Saijah drew her Dragon Talon bow to the cheek, nocking a steel-tipped incinerary shaft. She loosed with Master Archer precision, the arrow driving deep into the troll's chest and igniting where it struck.

Alfonso crossed the distance, his two-faced sword and heavy mace striking in a crossed flurry, carving fresh wounds through the broken hide.

The troll lunged blindly through the smoke, swinging a massive forearm toward Nora. Gear threw his armored frame into the blow, the impact sending metal screaming against the stone and buckling a seam of his plating.

PART V: TOO MUCH ARM

Varon dashed low along the cave wall, his blade coated in green-black Hist Sap toxin. He drove the steel into the troll's calf. The poison took hold instantly, clouding the beast's milky eyes with violent hallucinations. It swung wildly at empty air, roaring at phantoms in the snow.

Orion summoned a crackling Storm Atronach to the front line while Nora hurled another focused Firebolt into the creature's ribs.

The combined assault finally brought the beast down.

The beast collapsed to both knees in the drift, its head hanging. Then the black sap in its flesh began to boil.

The wounds across its torso did not merely close--they sprouted new tissue. A pale, hairless arm erupted from beneath its left shoulder. A second arm tore out of its spine, followed by two more from its ribcage, the raw muscle flexing in the cold air.

The troll rose to its full height with four arms tearing at the wind, the new flesh pale and unfinished where no hide had grown over it yet, and let out an unbroken, deafening roar.

Ylva's bones snapped and lengthened as her battleaxe fell to the snow. Fur sprouted across her shoulders, her jaw unhinging into a massive wolf snout as she transformed into full Werewolf Form, planting her claws in the red snow to hold the line.

The multi-armed monster turned on them, its extra limbs clawing the cavern walls, as the company braced for the creature's renewed fury.

PART VI: THE RELAY

The troll came for Ylva first and didn't stay. She swung as it passed — a short, ugly miss, her axe catching only air — and then it was gone, past her, bearing down on Orion where he stood between it and the Storm Atronach he'd called.

Orion raised his horn. The note came out low and steady, and he set his feet and did not move from where he stood.

The Atronach answered for him. A branch of lightning tore out of its crackling frame and caught the troll across one of its new, unfinished arms, and where it landed the raw muscle blackened and split, the corruption beneath it flinching visibly away from the light.

Nora was already gone from where she'd been standing — a shape of bent air near the rocks, nothing more — and when the troll's ruined arm brushed close to where she'd vanished, something answered it. A small violence, felt rather than seen, needled through the wound the lightning had already opened.

Ismara stepped clear of the horses. She didn't draw a blade. She raised one hand instead, and behind her, hovering where Alfonso's two-faced sword had been left driven point-down in the frost, the steel rose and turned in the air like something remembering how to be alive.

It found the troll's flank in a single continuous motion — in, out, around, in again — cutting the same wound wider each time it passed, four strokes that never touched her hand once. She stood with her arm still raised long after the blade had finished, watching the wounds she'd opened with the flat, assessing patience of someone reading a ledger.

Varon fired from the rocks. The first bolt found a gap in one of the troll's new limbs and stayed there. The second went wide, cracking off stone, and he was already reloading before it landed.

By the time the troll turned to see what had been doing this to it, there was very little left standing between the thing and the damage arriving on its skin.

It chose Orion.

The blow came in under his guard, and he didn't have time to do anything with the horn note still caught in his throat — it drove him back into the stone wall hard enough that the mountain answered with a groan of its own, and for a moment he didn't move.

He was still finding his feet when it hit him again, faster than anything that size should have been able to move twice in the same breath, and this time he went down and stayed down, blood dark against the snow beneath him.

The troll rounded on Alfonso next. It caught him full in the chest, and his obsidian ribs didn't dent — they came apart, bone by carved bone, clattering into the drift in a spray of black fragments, the light behind his eye sockets guttering out mid-motion. What was left of him lay scattered and still for the length of one long breath. Then, piece by piece, patient as a man reassembling a puzzle he'd solved a hundred times before, he began pulling himself back together.

The Storm Atronach took the last of it. One of the troll's new arms caught the elemental full in its crackling mass and drove it into the rock face hard enough to crack the stone, and for a moment the Atronach's light dimmed to almost nothing — a candle about to go out. It held. Barely. And where the troll's raw flesh had touched the current still humming through the Atronach's body, smoke rose off the wound, hissing.

Varon crossed the ground to Orion without being asked. Two vials went into him in quick succession, and by the second one Orion's breathing had steadied enough that he could put a hand flat against the stone and push himself upright.

PART VII: THE FALCON'S STRIKE

While the others held the line, Saijah had eyes only for the back wall of the den — smooth where the rest of the cave was jagged, a seam of stone that didn't belong to the mountain around it. She filed it away without a word and turned back to the fight.

Her hand went to her pack.

She'd carried the bottles since the caravan, the way a debt gets carried — quiet, unspoken, waiting for the moment it would matter more than the reasons not to touch it. This was that moment, or she'd decided it was, and there was no difference anymore between the two.

The skooma hit the way it had the first time, except larger — the world went soft at its edges and impossibly clear at its center, color bleeding into her vision in slow drifting motes of purple and green, and underneath the haze a well opened up that had no bottom to it at all. She could have flown to the top of the mountain and back without feeling the distance.

She went up instead. A short burst of flight took her along the cave wall and over the troll's ruined shoulder, and from there she dove — bow already drawn, an incinerary shaft nocked and burning at the tip before she'd even leveled the shot. She struck it running down the length of her dive, point-blank into the worst of the wounds the others had already opened, and the arrow didn't just hit. It found every gap the sunders had carved into the thing's hide and split them wider on the way through, the fire behind it going off like something that had been waiting its whole existence for exactly this target.

The troll's scream filled the tunnel. Whatever it had left in that ruined shoulder came apart in the blast, chunks of blackened flesh raining into the snow.

Saijah landed in a crouch some distance off, breathing hard, grinning at nothing, her eyes too bright.

Ylva didn't wait for an opening. She leapt the last of the distance in wolf form, claws leading, and raked a line down the creature's flank before it could turn to find her.

Ismara kept working. The floating blade came around again, and again, cutting into what little armor the troll had managed to regrow, patient as before — and beside her, Alfonso reassembled himself fully upright, raised both hands, and called the same white fire he'd used on Davinia's hand back in the monastery, except larger, and without mercy in it. It caught along the troll's exposed muscle and did not behave like ordinary flame — it ate.

PART VIII: WHAT THE TROLL REACHED FOR

The troll staggered back from the fire, and its ruined hand came down into the furrow at the mouth of the den — the same furrow where Mable's blood had frozen into the stone.

It closed its fingers around what was left of her.

Bjorn had been braced against the wall, watching, waiting for an opening. He saw the troll's fist close around the grey hide and the pale, familiar shape of a leg, saw it start to draw the remains toward itself the way it had drawn its own torn flesh back into its body twice already that day, and something in him that had held for forty years simply stopped holding.

He didn't shout. He crossed the ground between them at a dead run, put his shoulder into the creature's ribs with the full weight of what the Steed Stone had made him, and the impact staggered it backward off its feet, its grip on Mable tearing loose in the process. He didn't stop moving. A second strike, open-handed, cracked into the same ribs before the troll had finished falling.

It rounded on him for that. The blow it threw was meant for him and it never reached him — Ylva was between them a heartbeat too late to take it clean, and it caught her instead, flinging her back into the drift where she didn't get up.

"YLVA!"

Bjorn didn't look to see who else was close enough. He was already moving, already between the troll and where she'd fallen, and the second blow that had been meant to finish her caught him instead, square in the chest, a full measure of everything the creature had left. It should have put him down beside her. He stayed on his feet through it by a margin so thin he could feel his own heartbeat in his teeth, and did not step back.

Varon reached Ylva at a dead sprint, a potion already uncorked, and had it down her throat before her stillness had a chance to mean anything permanent. Her chest rose. Her eyes opened. He was moving again before she'd finished blinking, in low under the troll's guard, and opened its throat with a single motion that made the thing stumble.

Nora took the next blow meant for the line, and went down hard enough that for one terrible second nobody moved — and then her hand twitched, closed into a fist, and she came back up on her own, gasping, the last of whatever reserve she'd been carrying spent all at once.

The troll was staggering now, most of its stolen limbs hanging useless, its hide a ruin of black and raw pink where the fire and the blade and the poison had all found their marks in turn.

Saijah rose a second time, the skooma still roaring through her, and this dive was worse than the first — not louder, not faster, just further, like she'd stopped measuring the distance between herself and the ground at all. She came down out of the smoke with the bow already loosed, and the last arrow went through what remained of the troll's chest and out the other side in a column of fire that lit the whole den white for one instant.

The beast folded. It did not stand back up.

PART IX: WHAT WAS LEFT

The mutated mass collapsed into the snow, and something in the air went out of it all at once — the wrongness the party had been fighting since the wind tunnel, gone in a single breath. The black sap still pooling from its wounds hissed as it hit the frozen ground, already starting to skin over into something glassy and dark.

For a moment nobody spoke. The wind screamed on outside the overhang. Inside, everything was suddenly, deeply still.

Orion was the first to move. He knelt beside the body, drew a soul gem from his coat, and the light of the troll's passing soul poured into it without resistance — a second one now, sitting beside the first, both taken from the same god-warped thing that had killed Mable.

Ismara crossed to the pooling sap before it could freeze into the drift for good. She knelt, worked something slow and careful between her palms, and a container of permafrost rose up around the black liquid in clean, deliberate sheets of ice, sealing it before it could touch the snow.

Bjorn hadn't moved from where he'd fallen to his knees. He had Mable's recovered remains held against his chest — what little of her the troll hadn't already taken — and he wasn't making any sound at all, which was somehow worse than if he had been. Mila crossed the cave floor without being told to, small against the wreckage around her, and put her arms around his neck. He didn't look up. He didn't let go of the horse.

Saijah stood apart from the rest of it, still coming down off a height none of them could follow her to, her hands not quite steady, her eyes still too bright for the dim light of the cave. She looked at the frozen wall at the back of the den — the one she'd clocked mid-fight, the one that didn't belong to the mountain — and said nothing about it yet.

Nobody suggested moving further tonight. There was a door in the back of this cave that would still be there tomorrow, and a threshold that would still ask the same thing of them whenever they were ready to answer it.

For now, there was just the cold, and the quiet, and the work of counting who was still breathing.
